      "claim": "Valbenazine, a dimethoxytetrahydroisoquinoline-containing drug, has absolute oral bioavailability of approximately 49% in humans, supporting F >= 20%.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C34"],
      "claim": "Colchicine, a trimethoxy-substituted fused-ring drug, has mean absolute oral bioavailability of 55% in elderly subjects, supporting F >= 20%.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "Clomipramine, a tricyclic antidepressant with a dimethylaminopropyl side chain, has oral bioavailability of about 50% in humans, supporting F >= 20%.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C22"],
      "claim": "Donepezil, a dimethoxy-substituted drug with a basic amine, has oral bioavailability approaching 100%, supporting F >= 20%.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C15"],
      "claim": "Alfuzosin, a moderately lipophilic basic drug, has absolute oral bioavailability of 64.5% in healthy subjects, supporting F >= 20%.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "S-verapamil during rifampicin co-treatment has absolute oral bioavailability of 0.7%, but this reflects a drug-drug interaction and is not applicable to baseline oral bioavailability.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "Multiple structurally related analogues with high transfer scores show absolute oral bioavailability of 30-100%, consistently supporting F >= 20%. The only low-F record is a rifampin drug-interaction condition and is not applicable to baseline. The physicochemical prior is mixed but low confidence, and the experimental evidence outweighs it. Therefore predict high.",
